Refs stored in a dynamic-array slice
====================================

`new [n]&T` allocates a heap slice whose cells hold `&T`
references. Each cell starts as a null ref (zero-init); writes
of `new T(...)` populate cells with owning refs.

The refcount obligations on a slice-of-refs are subtle:

  - **Write**: `xs[i] = r` must increment `r`'s refcount AND
    decrement the previous occupant of `xs[i]` (if any). A
    naive "just store the pointer" causes double-frees on
    drop.
  - **Read**: `xs[i]` reads the ref *without* taking
    ownership; the refcount stays the same.
  - **Drop**: when the slice itself is dropped (binding goes
    out of scope, or `xs` is reassigned), every cell that
    holds a live ref must decrement that ref's refcount, then
    the underlying buffer is freed. A cell holding a null ref
    must not be decremented.
  - **Sub-slice**: `xs[a:b]` produces a view that shares
    cells with the parent. Reads through the view see the
    same data. The current sysl spec doesn't require sub-
    slices to retain their parent (no "lifetime extension"),
    so this file only pins the **shared-storage** observation
    via parent-then-child reads.

The bug class to surface: a backend that doesn't iterate
slice cells on drop, or that decrements cells holding null
refs, or that fails to incr-on-write.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct RisBox
        v: int

Basic write-then-read of refs in a slice
----------------------------------------

Each cell receives an owned `new RisBox(...)`. The reads see
the right v. The slice goes out of scope at fn exit; the
backing buffer + each cell's ref must drop cleanly. A bug
that leaks the cells wouldn't be visible from this test
alone, but a bug that double-frees or decrements null refs
would corrupt the heap and the *next* allocation traps.

    #test
    test_slice_of_refs_basic_write_read() -> unit
        val xs: &[]&RisBox = new [3]&RisBox
        xs[0] = new RisBox(10)
        xs[1] = new RisBox(20)
        xs[2] = new RisBox(30)
        assert(xs[0].v == 10, s"xs[0].v should be 10, got ${xs[0].v}")
        assert(xs[1].v == 20, s"xs[1].v should be 20, got ${xs[1].v}")
        assert(xs[2].v == 30, s"xs[2].v should be 30, got ${xs[2].v}")

Allocating, dropping, re-allocating — heap not corrupted
---------------------------------------------------------

After a slice-of-refs goes out of scope, the next allocation
should succeed unchanged. Repeats N times to stress
allocator state — any double-free or use-after-free in the
drop path would taint the freelist and a subsequent malloc
would return a stale or null pointer.

    #test
    test_slice_of_refs_alloc_drop_loop() -> unit
        var i = 0
        while i < 20
            val xs: &[]&RisBox = new [5]&RisBox
            var j = 0
            while j < 5
                xs[j] = new RisBox(i * 10 + j)
                j = j + 1
            // Verify the writes are consistent
            assert(xs[0].v == i * 10, s"xs[0] should be ${i * 10}, got ${xs[0].v}")
            assert(xs[4].v == i * 10 + 4, s"xs[4] should be ${i * 10 + 4}, got ${xs[4].v}")
            i = i + 1
        // If 20 iterations completed without trap, the drop path is sound.
        assert(true, "completed 20 alloc/drop cycles")

Reassigning a cell drops the old ref
------------------------------------

`xs[0] = new RisBox(...)` replaces the existing occupant.
The OLD ref's refcount must drop (and the old box gets freed
if it was the only owner). A bug that skips the decrement
leaks the old box; a bug that double-decrements double-frees.

    #test
    test_cell_reassign_drops_old() -> unit
        val xs: &[]&RisBox = new [1]&RisBox
        xs[0] = new RisBox(1)
        xs[0] = new RisBox(2)
        xs[0] = new RisBox(3)
        // If any reassignment double-freed, subsequent allocations
        // would hit corrupted heap; the read here would trap.
        assert(xs[0].v == 3, s"after three reassigns, xs[0].v should be 3, got ${xs[0].v}")

Mutation through a cell visible after read-out
----------------------------------------------

`val r = xs[i]` reads the ref. Mutating through `r` is
visible through `xs[i]` because both bindings point to the
same box. Pins that cell-reads share storage, not copy.

    #test
    test_cell_read_shares_storage() -> unit
        val xs: &[]&RisBox = new [1]&RisBox
        xs[0] = new RisBox(7)
        val r: &RisBox = xs[0]
        r.v = 99
        assert(xs[0].v == 99, s"mutation through aliased ref should be visible in slice; got ${xs[0].v}")

Sub-slice shares storage with parent
------------------------------------

`xs[a:b]` is a view, not a copy. Mutating through the view
is visible through the parent and vice-versa. The slice
header (ptr+len+cap) is a value-typed descriptor, but the
underlying backing buffer is shared.

    #test
    test_sub_slice_shares_cells() -> unit
        val xs: &[]&RisBox = new [3]&RisBox
        xs[0] = new RisBox(1)
        xs[1] = new RisBox(2)
        xs[2] = new RisBox(3)
        val view = xs[1:3]
        assert(len(view) == 2, s"view len should be 2, got ${len(view)}")
        assert(view[0].v == 2, s"view[0] aliases xs[1]; expected 2, got ${view[0].v}")
        // Mutate through the view; visible in parent.
        view[0].v = 42
        assert(xs[1].v == 42, s"parent xs[1] should see view's mutation; got ${xs[1].v}")

Two slice variables sharing the same underlying buffer
------------------------------------------------------

When a slice binding is copied to another binding of the
same type, both bindings see the same data. Pins that the
slice descriptor's underlying buffer is shared (not deep-
copied) — same as the sub-slice case but without indexing.

    #test
    test_slice_binding_copy_shares() -> unit
        val a: &[]&RisBox = new [2]&RisBox
        a[0] = new RisBox(10)
        a[1] = new RisBox(20)
        val b: &[]&RisBox = a
        assert(b[0].v == 10, s"b[0] aliases a[0]; expected 10, got ${b[0].v}")
        b[1].v = 200
        assert(a[1].v == 200, s"a[1] should see b's mutation; got ${a[1].v}")

Many writes to same cell — no leak (heap probe)
-----------------------------------------------

A single cell, written many times with fresh `new RisBox`.
Each write must drop the previous occupant. If the drop is
skipped, allocating 1000 boxes one-by-one would exhaust
memory. The test runs 200 writes which is enough to surface
any allocator-state corruption.

    #test
    test_single_cell_repeated_write() -> unit
        val xs: &[]&RisBox = new [1]&RisBox
        var i = 0
        while i < 200
            xs[i % 1] = new RisBox(i)
            i = i + 1
        assert(xs[0].v == 199, s"final xs[0] should be 199, got ${xs[0].v}")
